    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The Monogram pattern dates back to 1896, designed by George Vuitton, the brand's second-generation heir. It draws inspiration from a diverse range of sources, including the kitchen tiles of the Asnières family estate, neo-Gothic art, and Japanese motifs. The pattern weaves together LV initials and floral roundels, and can be printed or embossed on any surface—canvas, leather, faux leather, or paper. It quickly became the brand's signature, officially patented in 1897. Originally a safeguard against counterfeiting, it has since evolved into an enduring symbol of the house.",
      "url": null,
      "full_url": null,
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "text": null,
      "url": "https://cdn.kynbag.com/images/pax7od3/7dc93b8b4401da27.jpg",
      "full_url": "https://cdn.kynbag.com/images/pax7od3/61b3c41c0af86d8d.jpg",
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The bag's sides are adorned with VVN leather trim, whose contrasting color highlights the silhouette while providing reinforcement and structure. VVN stands for \"Vache Végétale Naturelle,\" a natural, untreated cowhide that undergoes vegetable tanning and meticulous cutting to preserve its raw grain and supple texture. Over time, it develops a warm patina.",
      "url": null,
      "full_url": null,
      "alt": null,
      "columns": 2,
      "items": []
    },
    {
